# M48 (PLAN_BROWSER_PERF_SECURITY.md §2.20, D82) — rung F: a real full-text-search read against
# the bulk-seeded catalog (`GET /products?q=gadgetronic`, apiV2's `to_tsvector`/`plainto_tsquery`
# query), distinct from rung C's `GET /health` — genuine Postgres query cost on an uncontended,
# public, read-only path, not an indexed health check. `gadgetronic` is the stable search term
# testFlow-tests' own seed.ts deliberately plants in every 10th bulk item's description (12 hits
# of ~120 products — see testFlow-tests/tests/api/catalog/large-catalog.tflw, the functional test
# this term was first established for). No session: unlike every other dogfood rung, `GET /products`
# needs no auth at all (products.controller.ts: "Reads are public") — this rung deliberately skips
# the `as load` session tflw pays for elsewhere, to measure the endpoint's real unauthenticated cost.
# Same ramp shape as the rest of the ladder for apples-to-apples comparison.

test "M48 search-read"
  ramp to 60 users over 20s
  api GET /products?q=gadgetronic
  expect status equals 200
  # `TF033` (tflw M60): a workload-bearing test's verdict comes only from its thresholds, so one is
  # required. This rung measures rather than gates, so the bound is deliberately loose — but not
  # vacuous: the endpoint is public and read-only, so an error here is the server, not the load
  # shape. See perf/README.md for how this ladder is run and checked.
  threshold error rate is less than 1%
